# KNOWN GAP — CALLING a `Box[Callable[...]]` is a compiler ICE (rc 101,
# `call to undefined function @b`). The CONSTRUCTOR is fine; the CALL is not.
#
#     Box[Callable[int(int)]] b = Box.new(c.f)
#     print(b(5))            # <- rc 101, call to undefined function @b
#     print(1)               # <- identical ctor, NO call: rc 0
#
# DISCRIMINATOR — measured by holding the ctor FIXED and varying only whether
# the box is called. All four ctor spellings ICE *when followed by a call* and
# build clean *without* one:
#     Box.new(c.f) · Box.new(c) · Box[Callable[int(int)]](c) · Box.new(dbl)
# A closure LITERAL source + a call works (rc 0).
#
# DISTINCT FROM `t0681`, its sibling in the same family — state the
# discriminator before merging them:
#   * `t0681` is a SPELLING-axis defect: `Box[T](closure)` ICEs at
#     `src/ir/lowering/mod.rs:1724` while `Box.new(closure)` compiles and runs.
#     Its discriminator is WHICH CTOR SPELLING; the box is never called.
#   * THIS is a CALL-axis defect with a DIFFERENT panic (`call to undefined
#     function @b`, no `mod.rs:1724` frame). Its discriminator is WHETHER THE
#     BOX IS CALLED; every ctor spelling behaves the same.
#
# SCOPE NOTE: because the identical ctor builds clean without the call, the
# mechanism is at the CALL site, not the constructor position. This is the same
# reasoning that ejected `t0684` from R45 Track A -- it is NOT a Box-ctor
# payload cell, and folding it in as one would widen that track on a false
# premise.
#
# INTENDED (asserted below): prints 10.
# Measured 2026-08-27 at HEAD; orchestrator-verified.

struct C:
    Callable[int(int)] f

int dbl(int x):
    return x * 2

void main():
    C c = C(dbl)
    Box[Callable[int(int)]] b = Box.new(c.f)
    print(b(5))
